# Rank 1768 / CVE-2026-33151 (CWE-400/CWE-770): the socket.io wire protocol
# frames a packet that carries binary payloads as a short text header followed
# by that many separate frames -- `5` (binary event), the attachment count, `-`,
# then the JSON payload with each payload replaced by a
# `{"_placeholder":true,"num":N}` marker. The count is typed by the peer. On the
# receiving side the built-in parser's documented job is to hold every arriving
# frame until it has that many of them, so the count the client typed becomes
# the number of buffers the server retains:
#
#   function decodeString(str) {                    // socket.io-parser index.js
#     ...
#     while (str.charAt(++i) !== '-' && i != str.length) {}
#     var buf = str.substring(start, i);
#     if (buf != Number(buf) || str.charAt(i) !== '-') {
#       throw new Error('Illegal attachments');
#     }
#     p.attachments = Number(buf);                  // <-- the peer's count, stored
#   }
#
#   BinaryReconstructor.prototype.takeBinaryData = function(binData) {
#     this.buffers.push(binData);
#     if (this.buffers.length === this.reconPack.attachments) {  // <-- the bound
#       ...
#   Decoder.prototype.add = function(obj) {
#       ...
#       this.reconstructor = new BinaryReconstructor(packet);
#
# Nothing anywhere compares the count to a ceiling. The pre-existing
# `buf != Number(buf)` test only rejects text that is not numeric at all: it
# passes `-5` and `1e9` alike, so a header of a few bytes asks the server to
# retain an arbitrary number of frames, each of arbitrary size, for as long as
# the client cares to leave the packet half-finished. The advisory's own
# regression test in the fixing commit builds the attack as
# `"5" + 1e9 + '-["ev"]'`.
#
# 719f9eba ("fix(parser): add a limit to the number of binary attachments")
# adds that ceiling three ways at once: the `Decoder` constructor takes an
# options object (`maxAttachments`, default 10), `decodeString` receives it as
# a second parameter, and the count is checked before it is stored --
# `!isInteger(n) || n < 0` for the malformed cases and `n > maxAttachments`
# for the unbounded one, with the packet now taking the checked local
# (`p.attachments = n`) rather than the coercion. The row's SHA sits on
# `socket.io-parser/3.4.x` inside the socket.io monorepo, which is the 2.x
# CommonJS prototype-style parser; the same fix landed on `main` as b25738c41
# over the 4.x TypeScript class-based parser at packages/socket.io-parser, and
# the two files share the vulnerable line verbatim.
#
# What fires without this change. `timeout 180 ./bin/vyql scan --cache off --max-ram
# 6GB` over the whole checkout at `<fix>^` reports 3 findings, all
# VYQL-INJ-013 on binary.js's `_deconstructPacket`/`_reconstructPacket`
# placeholder walks, and nothing at all on index.js. `vyql match` labels 4
# `code.Import` nodes and three `code.DomInput` params; the attachment count,
# the reconstructor and the completion test are unlabelled. Findings in the
# package are not coverage.
#
# code.UnboundedAllocationReview is reused rather than minted, and this is the
# concept's own language -- "review allocation sizes derived from parsed
# counts" -- where the parsed count is the attachment count read out of the
# packet header and the allocation is the frame list held until it is met.
# Ranks 1381 (a client picks a GraphQL selection set's nesting depth) and 1412
# (a client picks a form field name's bracket depth) established it as the home
# for exactly this judgement; here a client picks the retention bound. Also
# considered and rejected: code.UnboundedResourceOperation reads "long-lived
# resource operation ... timeouts, message limits, cancellation", which a
# bounded-wait accumulation is not; code.UnderboundedProtocolCountLimit is
# about a limit that exists but is set too low, and here there is no limit at
# all; code.OversizedWebsocketPayloadDefault is socket.io's *payload size*
# default (rank 755), a different axis -- the frame size is capped elsewhere
# and does not help, because the count is what is unbounded. No new concept and
# no new rule ID; the binding emits into VYQL-DOS-041.
#
# The binding is module-scope, which is where the vocabulary is: the header
# decode, the reconstructor and the completion test live in three different
# functions, and a ceiling is configured on the decoder's constructor, a fourth
# scope. The coercion is the load-bearing positive -- `attachments =
# Number(buf)` is the point where text the peer typed becomes the integer that
# sizes the retention, and it is shared verbatim by the 2.x CommonJS and 4.x
# TypeScript implementations of the protocol, so the binding reports both
# pre-fix revisions and neither fixed one. Measured on the extracted files:
# 1/0 on 2.x across the fixed revision and 1/0 on 4.x across b25738c41, for the identical
# reason.
#
# The fixed revision's own vocabulary is not the discriminator, tested by renaming rather
# than argued. Rewriting the patched 2.x revision with `maxAttachments`
# renamed to `zzQuantum` still reports 0, because `p.attachments = n` does not
# stores the coercion directly; dropping the coercion clause from the binding
# makes that same renamed revision report 1, so the coercion clause is what
# carries it. The negations carry the other direction: a revision that keeps
# `p.attachments = Number(buf)` and adds a named ceiling (`attachmentLimit`,
# `frameCap`, any local spelling `max*`/`*limit*`/`*cap*`) does not report,
# whether the ceiling arrives as a member read, a local, or an operand of the
# comparison that applies it. The honest limit in both directions: a ceiling
# named with none of those words and applied to a re-coerced copy
# (`var n = Number(buf); if (n > 10) throw; p.attachments = Number(buf)`) still
# reports -- no real revision of this parser is written that way, both actual
# fixes store the checked local -- and a module that merely *mentions* a
# ceiling-shaped word for an unrelated reason is silenced, which costs recall
# and is the safe direction.
#
# Collateral is enumerated rather than sampled, because a three-file library
# gives a vacuous zero. `attachments` as a property read together with a
# `.length ===` completion test and an `attachments = Number(` assignment
# appears in exactly one JavaScript or TypeScript file across every repository
# cloned for this campaign: this one.
#
# Measurement note. `vyql match` reads the persistent scan cache and cannot
# take `--cache off`, and a stale entry served a label set from an earlier
# definition state -- `containsAny(node.context.identifier, ...)` read as
# never-matching on this file until `vyql cache clear`, after which it matched
# the real identifier and rejected an absent one. Every count above is taken
# after `vyql cache clear`.

test "rank1768: the socket.io decoder stores the peer's attachment count straight from the coercion and buffers until it is met"
  lang javascript
  file index.js
  expect VYQL-DOS-041
  expect_review code.UnboundedAllocationReview
  code
  ```
  var Emitter = require('component-emitter');

  exports.BINARY_EVENT = 5;
  exports.BINARY_ACK = 6;

  exports.Decoder = Decoder;

  function Decoder() {
    this.reconstructor = null;
  }

  Emitter(Decoder.prototype);

  Decoder.prototype.add = function(obj) {
    var packet;
    if (typeof obj === 'string') {
      if (this.reconstructor) {
        throw new Error("got plaintext data when reconstructing a packet");
      }
      packet = decodeString(obj);
      if (exports.BINARY_EVENT === packet.type || exports.BINARY_ACK === packet.type) {
        this.reconstructor = new BinaryReconstructor(packet);

        if (this.reconstructor.reconPack.attachments === 0) {
          this.emit('decoded', packet);
        }
      } else {
        this.emit('decoded', packet);
      }
    } else if (isBuf(obj) || obj.base64) {
      if (!this.reconstructor) {
        throw new Error('got binary data when not reconstructing a packet');
      } else {
        packet = this.reconstructor.takeBinaryData(obj);
        if (packet) {
          this.reconstructor = null;
          this.emit('decoded', packet);
        }
      }
    } else {
      throw new Error('Unknown type: ' + obj);
    }
  };

  function isBuf(obj) {
    return obj instanceof Buffer;
  }

  function decodeString(str) {
    var i = 0;
    var p = {
      type: Number(str.charAt(0))
    };

    if (exports.BINARY_EVENT === p.type || exports.BINARY_ACK === p.type) {
      var start = i + 1;
      while (str.charAt(++i) !== '-' && i != str.length) {}
      var buf = str.substring(start, i);
      if (buf != Number(buf) || str.charAt(i) !== '-') {
        throw new Error('Illegal attachments');
      }
      p.attachments = Number(buf);
    }

    if ('/' === str.charAt(i + 1)) {
      p.nsp = '/';
    } else {
      p.nsp = '/';
    }

    return p;
  }

  function BinaryReconstructor(packet) {
    this.reconPack = packet;
    this.buffers = [];
  }

  BinaryReconstructor.prototype.takeBinaryData = function(binData) {
    this.buffers.push(binData);
    if (this.buffers.length === this.reconPack.attachments) {
      var packet = this.reconPack;
      this.finishedReconstruction();
      return packet;
    }
    return null;
  };

  BinaryReconstructor.prototype.finishedReconstruction = function() {
    this.reconPack = null;
    this.buffers = [];
  };
  ```

test "rank1768: the same decoder bounding the count against a configured ceiling before storing it"
  lang javascript
  file index.js
  reject VYQL-DOS-041
  reject_review code.UnboundedAllocationReview
  code
  ```
  var Emitter = require('component-emitter');

  exports.BINARY_EVENT = 5;
  exports.BINARY_ACK = 6;

  exports.Decoder = Decoder;

  function Decoder(opts) {
    this.reconstructor = null;
    opts = opts || {};
    this.opts = {
      maxAttachments: opts.maxAttachments || 10,
    };
  }

  Emitter(Decoder.prototype);

  Decoder.prototype.add = function(obj) {
    var packet;
    if (typeof obj === 'string') {
      if (this.reconstructor) {
        throw new Error("got plaintext data when reconstructing a packet");
      }
      packet = decodeString(obj, this.opts.maxAttachments);
      if (exports.BINARY_EVENT === packet.type || exports.BINARY_ACK === packet.type) {
        this.reconstructor = new BinaryReconstructor(packet);

        if (this.reconstructor.reconPack.attachments === 0) {
          this.emit('decoded', packet);
        }
      } else {
        this.emit('decoded', packet);
      }
    } else if (isBuf(obj) || obj.base64) {
      if (!this.reconstructor) {
        throw new Error('got binary data when not reconstructing a packet');
      } else {
        packet = this.reconstructor.takeBinaryData(obj);
        if (packet) {
          this.reconstructor = null;
          this.emit('decoded', packet);
        }
      }
    } else {
      throw new Error('Unknown type: ' + obj);
    }
  };

  function isBuf(obj) {
    return obj instanceof Buffer;
  }

  function decodeString(str, maxAttachments) {
    var i = 0;
    var p = {
      type: Number(str.charAt(0))
    };

    if (exports.BINARY_EVENT === p.type || exports.BINARY_ACK === p.type) {
      var start = i + 1;
      while (str.charAt(++i) !== '-' && i != str.length) {}
      var buf = str.substring(start, i);
      if (buf != Number(buf) || str.charAt(i) !== '-') {
        throw new Error('Illegal attachments');
      }
      var n = Number(buf);
      if (!isInteger(n) || n < 0) {
        throw new Error("Illegal attachments");
      } else if (n > maxAttachments) {
        throw new Error("too many attachments");
      }
      p.attachments = n;
    }

    if ('/' === str.charAt(i + 1)) {
      p.nsp = '/';
    } else {
      p.nsp = '/';
    }

    return p;
  }

  function BinaryReconstructor(packet) {
    this.reconPack = packet;
    this.buffers = [];
  }

  BinaryReconstructor.prototype.takeBinaryData = function(binData) {
    this.buffers.push(binData);
    if (this.buffers.length === this.reconPack.attachments) {
      var packet = this.reconPack;
      this.finishedReconstruction();
      return packet;
    }
    return null;
  };

  BinaryReconstructor.prototype.finishedReconstruction = function() {
    this.reconPack = null;
    this.buffers = [];
  };
  ```

test "rank1768: the same bound applied through a ceiling named without the usual spellings"
  lang javascript
  file index.js
  reject_review code.UnboundedAllocationReview
  code
  ```
  var Emitter = require('component-emitter');

  exports.BINARY_EVENT = 5;
  exports.BINARY_ACK = 6;

  exports.Decoder = Decoder;

  function Decoder(opts) {
    this.reconstructor = null;
    opts = opts || {};
    this.opts = {
      allowed: opts.allowed || 10,
    };
  }

  Emitter(Decoder.prototype);

  Decoder.prototype.add = function(obj) {
    var packet;
    if (typeof obj === 'string') {
      if (this.reconstructor) {
        throw new Error("got plaintext data when reconstructing a packet");
      }
      packet = decodeString(obj, this.opts.allowed);
      if (exports.BINARY_EVENT === packet.type || exports.BINARY_ACK === packet.type) {
        this.reconstructor = new BinaryReconstructor(packet);

        if (this.reconstructor.reconPack.attachments === 0) {
          this.emit('decoded', packet);
        }
      } else {
        this.emit('decoded', packet);
      }
    }
  };

  function decodeString(str, allowed) {
    var i = 0;
    var p = {
      type: Number(str.charAt(0))
    };

    if (exports.BINARY_EVENT === p.type || exports.BINARY_ACK === p.type) {
      var start = i + 1;
      while (str.charAt(++i) !== '-' && i != str.length) {}
      var buf = str.substring(start, i);
      if (buf != Number(buf) || str.charAt(i) !== '-') {
        throw new Error('Illegal attachments');
      }
      var n = Number(buf);
      if (!isInteger(n) || n < 0) {
        throw new Error("Illegal attachments");
      } else if (n > allowed) {
        throw new Error("too many attachments");
      }
      p.attachments = n;
    }

    return p;
  }

  function BinaryReconstructor(packet) {
    this.reconPack = packet;
    this.buffers = [];
  }

  BinaryReconstructor.prototype.takeBinaryData = function(binData) {
    this.buffers.push(binData);
    if (this.buffers.length === this.reconPack.attachments) {
      var packet = this.reconPack;
      this.finishedReconstruction();
      return packet;
    }
    return null;
  };

  BinaryReconstructor.prototype.finishedReconstruction = function() {
    this.reconPack = null;
    this.buffers = [];
  };
  ```

test "rank1768: an attachment list the application fills itself, with no count read off the wire"
  lang javascript
  file attachments.js
  reject_review code.UnboundedAllocationReview
  code
  ```
  var attachments = [];
  var expected = 0;

  function setExpected(count) {
    expected = count;
  }

  function addAttachment(file) {
    attachments.push(file);
    if (attachments.length === expected) {
      return attachments;
    }
    return null;
  }

  function packetFor(count) {
    var p = { type: 5 };
    p.attachments = count;
    return p;
  }
  ```
